        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: keep per-client caller sessions",
        "body": "## Summary\n\n- keep one caller credential per named client instead of rotating the account-wide credential on every login\n- preserve existing callers as a `legacy` client during migration\n- attribute audit, CLI stats, and dashboard traffic to the originating client\n- cap active clients at 16, retiring the least recently updated session without losing its historical attribution\n\n## Why\n\nThe caller model stored one token hash per GitHub user. Logging in from another machine replaced that hash, so only the most recently logged-in client remained authenticated. That also made per-machine usage and fallback analysis impossible.\n\n## Strategy\n\nThe login API now accepts a validated client name, with the CLI defaulting to the local hostname. Re-login rotates only the matching client. Authentication uses the per-client credential table, while audit events copy the immutable client name so session retirement cannot erase history or break in-flight audit writes.\n\nStats expose pool, caller, current-client, and per-client views. The operator dashboard adds the same client dimension. The 16-client LRU-style cap bounds abandoned hostnames and ephemeral sessions.\n\nThis makes the next rate-limit work measurable: compare cache/fallback/backend traffic by machine, converge clients on one deployment, then tune identities and high-miss Actions routes using unified evidence rather than aggregate guesses.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `pnpm check`\n- `pnpm test:e2e:cli-worker` reaches authenticated Worker routing; the local live-upstream proof remains environment-dependent\n- `pnpm docs:site`\n- legacy migration probe preserves the old token and audit attribution\n- production public-only fallback identity selected for a non-org check-runs request, returned HTTP 200, and recorded the selected identity in audit data\n- source-aware review clean after fixing migration backfill, fixture auth, session retention, and retired-session attribution findings; exact substantive commit rereviewed clean at 0.89 confidence\n",
